  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Streptococcus—Gram-positive, nonmotile, facultatively anaerobic spherical bacteria (cocci): ⚬ Commensal organisms; normal flora of the upper respiratory tract, oropharynx, lower genital tract, and skin. ⚬ Under appropriate conditions, capable of infecting all areas of body; frequent secondary invader of body tissues.",
              "pdf_page": 1381,
              "printed_page": 1299,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Classified by ability to hemolyze red blood cells on blood agar plates: ⚬ α-Hemolytic (green zone of partial hemolysis). ⚬ β-Hemolytic (clear zone of hemolysis); usually more pathogenic thanα-hemolytic, which is more pathogenic than nonhemolytic strains. ⚬ γ-Hemolytic (no change; nonhemolytic).",
              "pdf_page": 1381,
              "printed_page": 1299,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemolytic strains further subdivided by differences in cell walls (Lancefield serogroups A–H and K–T); some groups more likely to be associated with disease: ⚬ Group G is associated with cats and dogs; group A with humans). ⚬ Many group D streptococci reclassified as enterococci.",
              "pdf_page": 1381,
              "printed_page": 1299,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Produce exotoxins—streptolysins (hemolysins), streptokinases, deoxyribonucleases, and hyaluronidases.",
              "pdf_page": 1381,
              "printed_page": 1299,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Adhesins to bind extracellular matrix proteins.",
              "pdf_page": 1381,
              "printed_page": 1299,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
